# The WebUI serves its whole subscriber database over HTTP through
# express-restify-mongoose. One `serve(router, Model)` call per model
# publishes the collection and every document under it for GET, POST,
# PUT/PATCH and DELETE, plus a `query` parameter that takes arbitrary Mongo
# selectors -- so the served router is the database. The library documents
# that it applies no access control of its own: the options that add it are
# `access`, `contextFilter`, `preMiddleware`, `preCreate`, `preRead`,
# `preUpdate` and `preDelete`, and its authentication guidance is to mount the
# served router behind ordinary Express middleware.
#
# The routes index mounted it behind nothing. `router.use('/db', db)` sits one
# line below the login routes, so anyone who could reach the port could read
# every subscriber record, including the IMSI and the authentication keys, and
# write new ones. The session cookie the login form set is never consulted on
# that path.
#
# The scope is the module because that is the unit Express mounting happens
# in: `express.Router()` and the `use` calls that populate it sit together in
# a routes index, one file above the module that calls `serve`. Both are read
# as whole `call:` tokens rather than by receiver name, so `router.use`,
# `api.use` and `apiRouter.use` read alike.
#
# The last two cases say what the two halves of the binding are each doing.
# The third mounts the same database router behind a middleware passed by
# reference rather than invoked, which produces no call token at all -- the
# identifier naming the gate is what declines it, so the discrimination does
# not depend on the fixed revision's choice of passport. The fourth is the
# unguarded mount again in a project that does not depend on
# express-restify-mongoose: an Express router that mounts sub-routers is
# ordinary code, and without a library that turns a model into a public CRUD
# surface there is nothing here to report.

test "rank1498 open5gs: the webui routes index mounts the express-restify-mongoose database router with no authentication middleware"
